1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,550
-[Sitara cries]
-[SHIRA] Hey! Listen!

2
00:00:01,590 --> 00:00:04,380
You want to get that book back,
don't you?

3
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:10,140
[Sitara gasps softly]

4
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:12,940
Huh?

5
00:00:14,350 --> 00:00:16,940
[♪ opening theme song playing]

6
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:44,820
[battle cry]

7
00:01:46,240 --> 00:01:47,610
[SITARA (ADULT)]
The thirteenth century

8
00:01:47,780 --> 00:01:50,700
saw the rise of a vast empire
that swept across Eurasia.

9
00:01:51,370 --> 00:01:53,910
Genghis Khan,
the "universal ruler."

10
00:01:54,540 --> 00:01:56,750
He was hailed as the Great Khan,

11
00:01:56,790 --> 00:02:00,330
as he and his kin
built the Mongol Empire.

12
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:03,460
Although the Mongols were
originally a small,

13
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:06,800
nomadic tribe,
by the year 1206,

14
00:02:06,840 --> 00:02:09,380
they had conquered
the other steppe tribes

15
00:02:09,430 --> 00:02:11,300
and become a major power.

16
00:02:11,550 --> 00:02:15,220
A short time later, they began
advancing even further.

17
00:02:15,270 --> 00:02:17,310
Foraying into
the various nations

18
00:02:17,350 --> 00:02:18,810
that bordered the steppes.

19
00:02:23,230 --> 00:02:24,320
Greetings, my brothers.

20
00:02:24,360 --> 00:02:25,740
[ALL] Tolui.

21
00:02:27,950 --> 00:02:28,990
[slurps]

22
00:02:29,490 --> 00:02:30,910
Haven't you drunk enough?

23
00:02:31,870 --> 00:02:33,080
Welcome back.

24
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:36,620
[Tolui laughs]

25
00:02:37,250 --> 00:02:39,290
How was the Khwarazm campaign?

26
00:02:40,710 --> 00:02:42,670
It went well, except,

27
00:02:42,710 --> 00:02:45,210
we lost quite a few soldiers.

28
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:46,550
The shame is mine.

29
00:02:46,590 --> 00:02:48,260
Your indecision is to blame.

30
00:02:48,300 --> 00:02:50,930
You simply had to follow
Father's orders, Jochi.

31
00:02:50,970 --> 00:02:52,720
[ÖGEDEI] That's enough, you two.

32
00:02:53,050 --> 00:02:55,970
What about you, Tolui?
You were in Persia, right?

33
00:02:56,770 --> 00:03:00,600
Indeed. I brought honor to
Genghis Khan's name in battle.

34
00:03:00,650 --> 00:03:01,650
[laughs]

35
00:03:01,940 --> 00:03:04,900
Fitting for the Otchigin.
Our future emperor.

36
00:03:12,410 --> 00:03:15,200
[TOLUI] Not to mention,
I acquired something good.

37
00:03:18,120 --> 00:03:19,620
A foreign book, huh?

38
00:03:19,660 --> 00:03:21,540
I am told it's called Elements.

39
00:03:21,580 --> 00:03:22,580
♪

40
00:03:22,630 --> 00:03:24,130
[TOLUI] Before leaving home,

41
00:03:24,170 --> 00:03:26,090
my wife asked me for this book
as a token

42
00:03:26,130 --> 00:03:27,550
of my conquests.

43
00:03:27,590 --> 00:03:29,090
What a modest woman!

44
00:03:29,130 --> 00:03:30,590
After all,
she could have asked me

45
00:03:30,630 --> 00:03:33,510
to bring back gold
or rare jewels instead.

46
00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:36,220
She's so very special to me.

47
00:03:36,260 --> 00:03:38,520
I'd do anything just
to make her smile.

48
00:03:38,560 --> 00:03:39,890
[Ögedei whistles]

49
00:03:39,930 --> 00:03:41,600
So what's written
on these pages?

50
00:03:41,640 --> 00:03:42,810
[TOLUI] Hm?

51
00:03:44,980 --> 00:03:46,190
I have no clue.

52
00:03:46,230 --> 00:03:47,650
[ALL] Uh...

53
00:03:47,730 --> 00:03:51,110
Oh, so you have a scholar
who can explain its contents?

54
00:03:51,150 --> 00:03:52,160
[TOLUI] No.

55
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:53,700
Can your wife read tomes

56
00:03:53,740 --> 00:03:55,160
that are written
in foreign tongues?

57
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:57,830
[TOLUI] Not that I know of.
[laughs]

58
00:03:57,870 --> 00:03:59,200
[ÖGEDEI] Oh, dear.

59
00:04:02,040 --> 00:04:04,420
[SHIRA] See,
I'm planning on introducing you

60
00:04:04,460 --> 00:04:06,290
to Prince Tolui
as soon as possible.

61
00:04:07,040 --> 00:04:09,090
You're a scholar's daughter,
aren't you?

62
00:04:09,340 --> 00:04:11,550
I would've preferred
to volunteer myself,

63
00:04:11,590 --> 00:04:14,090
but I can only read
and write a few simple things.

64
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:17,100
I do pick up
new languages quickly, though.

65
00:04:18,890 --> 00:04:21,680
[SITARA] Why did she even
want that book, anyway?

66
00:04:21,730 --> 00:04:22,940
[SHIRA] Hm?

67
00:04:22,980 --> 00:04:25,610
-Uh, I don't know.
-She can't read the words.

68
00:04:25,650 --> 00:04:28,520
[SHIRA] And she asked for it
instead of gold or silver.

69
00:04:28,570 --> 00:04:30,110
Strange, to be sure.

70
00:04:30,610 --> 00:04:33,860
Still, this is your chance
to serve the imperial family.

71
00:04:33,900 --> 00:04:36,240
And to have that book back
in your hands again.

72
00:04:36,280 --> 00:04:38,370
Doesn't sound
like too bad a deal,

73
00:04:38,410 --> 00:04:40,370
wouldn't you say? [gasps]

74
00:04:41,290 --> 00:04:43,120
[SITARA] The Mongol emperor?

75
00:04:43,160 --> 00:04:45,000
Serve his family?

76
00:04:45,170 --> 00:04:48,460
No. That aside, worse still.

77
00:04:49,340 --> 00:04:52,550
[TOLUI] Before leaving home,
my wife asked me for this book.

78
00:04:55,630 --> 00:04:59,890
[SITARA] My mistress died
over such a trifle?

79
00:05:00,930 --> 00:05:05,060
So, um, what happened was
most unfortunate.

80
00:05:05,480 --> 00:05:07,440
And I do regret it.

81
00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:09,940
But I didn't have a choice.

82
00:05:09,980 --> 00:05:11,900
I'm doing everything I can

83
00:05:11,940 --> 00:05:13,570
to try and get
in their good favor.

84
00:05:13,610 --> 00:05:16,570
I don't ever want to be sent
to the front lines.

85
00:05:16,610 --> 00:05:19,280
Boys who are around my age
end up at the very front

86
00:05:19,320 --> 00:05:22,620
of their expedition armies...
as human shields.

87
00:05:22,660 --> 00:05:24,580
-[Sitara exhales]
-[battle cry]

88
00:05:25,080 --> 00:05:26,250
I see.

89
00:05:26,790 --> 00:05:28,960
You're too young
to grow a beard,

90
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:30,170
yet they took you?

91
00:05:30,210 --> 00:05:31,550
Yes, that's right.

92
00:05:31,590 --> 00:05:33,880
-No, enough talk of the past!
-[gasps softly]

93
00:05:33,920 --> 00:05:36,090
This is my big chance
to move up in the world.

94
00:05:36,130 --> 00:05:37,630
For the first time,

95
00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:40,890
a path to success based on merit
has opened up to us.

96
00:05:40,930 --> 00:05:43,720
I will do whatever it takes
to thrive and succeed.

97
00:05:43,770 --> 00:05:46,350
Now you can finally
make the most of your education.

98
00:05:46,390 --> 00:05:48,810
Well, that and your
fetching face.

99
00:05:48,860 --> 00:05:50,650
If all the stars become aligned,

100
00:05:50,690 --> 00:05:52,320
one day, you could become
an attendant

101
00:05:52,360 --> 00:05:53,820
to a high-ranking figure.

102
00:05:54,190 --> 00:05:55,280
Mm?

103
00:05:55,440 --> 00:05:58,570
Her smile is quite fetching,
as you can see!

104
00:05:59,700 --> 00:06:02,410
[SHIRA] That's the very reason
you ought to do something.

105
00:06:02,450 --> 00:06:04,160
But what exactly?

106
00:06:04,790 --> 00:06:06,210
How could I possibly know

107
00:06:06,250 --> 00:06:09,330
what course of action
I'm supposed to take here?

108
00:06:10,130 --> 00:06:11,790
What should I do?

109
00:06:15,800 --> 00:06:16,920
[MUHAMMAD]
But if one were to study

110
00:06:16,970 --> 00:06:18,630
and become wise,
then one would know

111
00:06:18,680 --> 00:06:20,220
the best course of action
to take,

112
00:06:20,260 --> 00:06:21,930
no matter what the problem
might be.

113
00:06:21,970 --> 00:06:23,220
That's always.

114
00:06:23,260 --> 00:06:24,720
[Sitara breathes shakily]

115
00:06:25,520 --> 00:06:26,600
[Sitara grunts]

116
00:06:28,060 --> 00:06:29,310
[SHIRA] Uh?

117
00:06:32,940 --> 00:06:34,270
♪

118
00:06:35,480 --> 00:06:36,610
[Shira exhales softly]

119
00:06:36,820 --> 00:06:39,030
[SITARA] I am well-versed
in ancient wisdom

120
00:06:39,070 --> 00:06:41,160
and modern teachings alike.

121
00:06:41,530 --> 00:06:43,030
Needless to say,

122
00:06:43,490 --> 00:06:46,200
I am able to read
Euclid's Elements.

123
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:49,460
And you're right.

124
00:06:51,130 --> 00:06:55,130
I want to lead a cushy life,
eating amazing food every day.

125
00:06:58,800 --> 00:07:00,760
This is my chance!

126
00:07:00,800 --> 00:07:03,890
If the both of us work together,
then we can make it happen!

127
00:07:03,930 --> 00:07:05,430
Uh, yeah.

128
00:07:06,220 --> 00:07:08,140
So, um, my name's Shira.

129
00:07:08,180 --> 00:07:10,020
-Mm?
-And I'm from Samarkand.

130
00:07:11,060 --> 00:07:12,770
My name's Sitara.

131
00:07:12,810 --> 00:07:14,360
Sitara?

132
00:07:14,400 --> 00:07:17,360
Hold on.
With a garish name like that...

133
00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:18,940
Yes, I am a slave.

134
00:07:18,990 --> 00:07:20,110
And what of it?

135
00:07:20,320 --> 00:07:21,990
[Shira sighs]

136
00:07:22,570 --> 00:07:24,370
[SHIRA]
We're in a new social order,

137
00:07:24,410 --> 00:07:26,950
but being well-born
still gets you a long way.

138
00:07:26,990 --> 00:07:30,710
Especially when dealing
with the imperial family.

139
00:07:30,750 --> 00:07:33,040
Well, you can just lie.

140
00:07:33,420 --> 00:07:35,300
Let's go with
a more dignified name.

141
00:07:35,340 --> 00:07:36,710
[Sitara chuckles softly]

142
00:07:37,090 --> 00:07:38,800
In that case...

143
00:07:51,940 --> 00:07:53,060
[in English] Listen,

144
00:07:53,310 --> 00:07:56,020
anybody who's granted
an audience with the Mongols

145
00:07:56,060 --> 00:07:58,150
must pass between two fires.

146
00:07:58,190 --> 00:07:59,320
'Cause they all believe

147
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:01,400
fire has the power
to purify evil.

148
00:08:02,320 --> 00:08:04,120
[SITARA] A heathen ritual, huh?

149
00:08:11,500 --> 00:08:13,250
[SHIRA] Also,
you must never step

150
00:08:13,290 --> 00:08:14,710
on the entrance's threshold.

151
00:08:15,250 --> 00:08:17,130
If you do,
you'll be put to death.

152
00:08:42,360 --> 00:08:44,150
[in English]
"Greetings, scholar's daughter.

153
00:08:44,200 --> 00:08:46,070
I am glad to see
you're still alive,

154
00:08:46,120 --> 00:08:47,870
so says His Highness,"
he says.

155
00:09:04,380 --> 00:09:05,930
[Sitara, in English]
My name is...

156
00:09:09,100 --> 00:09:11,890
Fatima, Your Highness.

157
00:09:11,930 --> 00:09:13,310
♪

158
00:09:19,020 --> 00:09:22,150
[SITARA (ADULT)] These fires
won't ever erase them.

159
00:09:24,150 --> 00:09:25,650
Not those tender days

160
00:09:25,700 --> 00:09:28,120
that only exist
in my memories now.

161
00:09:28,990 --> 00:09:32,750
Nor my ties to the mistress,
and the others.

162
00:09:33,910 --> 00:09:36,210
If there's one thing I know
I can still do,

163
00:09:36,250 --> 00:09:38,580
it's taking that book back.

164
00:09:39,460 --> 00:09:43,510
Until that day, I shall remain
my mistress' slave.

165
00:09:44,300 --> 00:09:46,170
[wind blows]

166
00:10:00,270 --> 00:10:02,860
Well, I have avoided a fate
on the front lines,

167
00:10:02,900 --> 00:10:05,110
thanks to the commander's
good word. Huh?

168
00:10:05,610 --> 00:10:07,280
Is she Tolui's wife?

169
00:10:07,320 --> 00:10:08,780
No. Princess Sorghaghtani

170
00:10:08,820 --> 00:10:10,870
should be at the Mongols'
home camp right now,

171
00:10:10,910 --> 00:10:12,620
so that definitely isn't her
over there.

172
00:10:13,330 --> 00:10:15,000
I wonder
who she could be, then.

173
00:10:15,040 --> 00:10:16,580
One of the generals' wives?

174
00:10:16,710 --> 00:10:17,790
[SITARA] What?

175
00:10:18,080 --> 00:10:20,500
The Mongols always bring
quite a few women with them,

176
00:10:20,540 --> 00:10:22,500
even out
on military expeditions.

177
00:10:22,540 --> 00:10:24,340
Women on an expedition?

178
00:10:24,710 --> 00:10:27,130
I can't even begin
to imagine it.

179
00:10:29,090 --> 00:10:32,100
First, I need to
learn about them.

180
00:10:32,140 --> 00:10:35,930
Shira, teach me some
simple Mongolian words.

181
00:10:35,980 --> 00:10:37,480
I want to know their language.

182
00:10:37,520 --> 00:10:40,640
Sure. In return, I ask that
you help me in times of need.

183
00:10:41,270 --> 00:10:42,520
Of course I will.

184
00:10:42,570 --> 00:10:44,820
We're both on the same side,
aren't we?

185
00:10:45,570 --> 00:10:49,570
Everybody here
is my sworn enemy.

186
00:10:51,240 --> 00:10:53,200
As alone as I may be...

187
00:10:53,240 --> 00:10:56,450
I'll fight to the bitter end.

188
00:11:06,670 --> 00:11:07,920
[hoofbeats]

189
00:11:07,970 --> 00:11:10,220
[ÖGEDEI]
So, there you are, Töregene.

190
00:11:10,260 --> 00:11:12,010
It's Prince Ögedei.

191
00:11:12,050 --> 00:11:13,760
[attendant gasps softly]

192
00:11:14,680 --> 00:11:17,310
What? No words of appreciation

193
00:11:17,350 --> 00:11:19,430
for your husband who's returned
from battle?

194
00:11:20,600 --> 00:11:22,310
[TÖREGENE]
You are mistaken.

195
00:11:22,360 --> 00:11:24,020
I am not your wife.

196
00:11:24,400 --> 00:11:26,230
I am your enemy.

197
00:11:35,700 --> 00:11:37,950
[SITARA (ADULT)]
The year is 1221.

198
00:11:38,250 --> 00:11:42,120
The Mongol army has been resting
for a short time in Taloqan.

199
00:11:43,380 --> 00:11:46,840
But now, they are setting out
once again in the autumn.

200
00:11:47,500 --> 00:11:50,840
The army heads south
for the Khwarazmian Empire.

201
00:11:51,420 --> 00:11:55,180
They intend to swiftly vanquish
the empire's last ruler.

202
00:11:56,600 --> 00:11:58,600
The conflict
that ultimately follows

203
00:11:58,640 --> 00:12:02,230
will go down in history
as the Battle of the Indus.

204
00:12:04,520 --> 00:12:06,650
Less noticed by history,

205
00:12:06,690 --> 00:12:09,320
is that those taken captive
in Persia

206
00:12:09,360 --> 00:12:12,860
have continued their long march
to the Mongol home camp.

207
00:12:12,900 --> 00:12:14,870
It's been half of a year.

208
00:12:14,910 --> 00:12:16,780
How much longer
will this march take?

209
00:12:16,830 --> 00:12:18,080
-[SOLDIER] Halt!
-[ALL] Hm?

210
00:12:18,120 --> 00:12:19,580
[all sigh]

211
00:12:20,870 --> 00:12:23,210
[SOLDIER] Begin constructing
the gers immediately!

212
00:12:23,250 --> 00:12:25,960
An honored guest of the
Great Khan is heading here,

213
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,090
traveling all the way
from the east.

214
00:12:28,130 --> 00:12:29,800
We are to set up camp
and provide him

215
00:12:29,840 --> 00:12:31,170
with lodging for the night.

216
00:12:31,220 --> 00:12:33,170
[cow moos]

217
00:12:33,220 --> 00:12:35,300
[indistinct chatter]

218
00:12:39,010 --> 00:12:41,100
-[SITARA] I help with this?
-[SOLDIER] Mm?

219
00:12:41,140 --> 00:12:44,270
No, this is a job for a man.

220
00:12:49,770 --> 00:12:51,440
Understand, yes.

221
00:12:53,450 --> 00:12:55,820
There's still much about
the Mongols' way of life

222
00:12:55,860 --> 00:12:57,740
that I find very puzzling.

223
00:12:57,780 --> 00:13:00,910
But I think I'm slowly
starting to understand it.

224
00:13:00,950 --> 00:13:02,040
♪

225
00:13:03,290 --> 00:13:06,170
They're all totally helpless
without me around.

226
00:13:06,210 --> 00:13:08,790
[SITARA] For example,
their tents are always built

227
00:13:08,840 --> 00:13:10,710
so that their entrances
are oriented south.

228
00:13:10,750 --> 00:13:13,170
Hey, you there! It's time
to water the livestock!

229
00:13:13,220 --> 00:13:14,720
[SITARA] In addition to that,

230
00:13:14,760 --> 00:13:17,470
they seem to follow
a rigorous daily schedule.

231
00:13:19,140 --> 00:13:20,640
[sighs]

232
00:13:20,680 --> 00:13:21,770
It makes me wonder

233
00:13:22,180 --> 00:13:25,140
how they're able to keep track
of the time so accurately.

234
00:13:27,150 --> 00:13:28,400
Uh?

235
00:13:31,980 --> 00:13:34,490
I see, a sundial...

236
00:13:34,530 --> 00:13:36,660
where the light falls
inside the house

237
00:13:36,700 --> 00:13:38,160
tells them what time it is.

238
00:13:39,120 --> 00:13:40,280
Hmm.

239
00:13:40,660 --> 00:13:43,500
It's quite clever to have
the whole house act as a clock.

240
00:13:43,540 --> 00:13:44,540
Uh?

241
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,540
Although, an astrolabe
would be able to tell the time

242
00:13:47,580 --> 00:13:48,960
more precisely.

243
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,630
This is nothing
but the wisdom of savages.

244
00:13:53,590 --> 00:13:55,630
[indistinct chatter]

245
00:14:00,180 --> 00:14:01,470
Have you heard?

246
00:14:01,510 --> 00:14:04,060
They say tonight's guest
is an Eastern sage

247
00:14:04,100 --> 00:14:06,100
who knows the secret
to immortality.

248
00:14:06,140 --> 00:14:07,310
Immortality?

249
00:14:07,350 --> 00:14:08,730
-[CHIEFTAIN] Excuse me.
-[ALL] Mm?

250
00:14:08,770 --> 00:14:09,940
Good evening, ladies.

251
00:14:09,980 --> 00:14:12,270
We are Uyghurs
from the city close to here.

252
00:14:12,900 --> 00:14:15,490
We come with an offering
for the master sage.

253
00:14:15,530 --> 00:14:17,320
Do you think you could show us
where he is?

254
00:14:17,360 --> 00:14:19,570
-Not me.
-I would be too scared.

255
00:14:19,620 --> 00:14:20,780
But why?

256
00:14:21,030 --> 00:14:24,290
[SITARA] The idea of an immortal
is rather creepy.

257
00:14:24,330 --> 00:14:25,830
But still...

258
00:14:28,120 --> 00:14:29,870
[SITARA]
Please follow this way.

259
00:14:30,210 --> 00:14:32,250
Huh? Oh, is that you, Chieftain?

260
00:14:32,290 --> 00:14:36,720
Oh-ho, Chinqai! I didn't realize
you were accompanying the sage.

261
00:14:36,760 --> 00:14:40,180
My apologies. I should call you
Lord Chinqai, shouldn't I?

262
00:14:40,220 --> 00:14:41,720
[laughs]
There's no need for that.

263
00:14:41,760 --> 00:14:44,060
You helped me out greatly
when I was a mere peddler.

264
00:14:44,640 --> 00:14:46,810
[CHIEFTAIN]
Gifts for the master sage.

265
00:14:46,850 --> 00:14:50,270
Wine, bread, and watermelon
from our city of Janbaliq.

266
00:14:50,310 --> 00:14:51,940
Oh, my! Watermelon!

267
00:14:51,980 --> 00:14:53,900
-These look wonderful.
-[Sitara exclaims]

268
00:14:53,940 --> 00:14:55,190
All right, go on ahead.

269
00:14:55,230 --> 00:14:56,320
[Sitara exclaims]

270
00:14:56,570 --> 00:14:58,780
[CHINQAI] Here.
You'll bring this in with them.

271
00:14:59,070 --> 00:15:01,700
Oh, but I... I don't...

272
00:15:02,660 --> 00:15:04,410
Go, now. Follow them.

273
00:15:04,450 --> 00:15:05,620
But...

274
00:15:06,040 --> 00:15:08,080
[breathes uneasily]

275
00:15:08,540 --> 00:15:09,960
[SITARA] An immortal?

276
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:12,880
Can someone like that
really exist?

277
00:15:12,920 --> 00:15:15,590
I'll have to go see the truth
for myself.

278
00:15:16,630 --> 00:15:17,760
[chuckles softly]

279
00:15:20,340 --> 00:15:21,970
Allow me to introduce you.

280
00:15:22,010 --> 00:15:23,720
He was personally invited here

281
00:15:23,760 --> 00:15:25,720
by the Great Khan
to share his wisdom,

282
00:15:25,760 --> 00:15:28,770
and is currently in the midst
of a long journey to the west.

283
00:15:28,810 --> 00:15:32,690
This is Master Changchun,
the highly respected sage.

284
00:15:32,730 --> 00:15:34,150
[SITARA] How scary!

285
00:15:34,190 --> 00:15:37,150
He looks like he's been alive
for over 200 years!

286
00:15:37,190 --> 00:15:39,490
-The offering, if you will.
-[Sitara gasps]

287
00:15:44,160 --> 00:15:46,580
[Changchun mumbles]

288
00:15:46,620 --> 00:15:48,040
[INTERPRETER] Hm.

289
00:15:48,080 --> 00:15:49,290
Mm-hmm.

290
00:15:49,330 --> 00:15:51,750
Master Changchun asks you
the following...

291
00:15:51,790 --> 00:15:54,330
"Did anyone here
see the solar eclipse

292
00:15:54,380 --> 00:15:56,210
on the first day
of the fifth month?"

293
00:15:56,250 --> 00:15:57,380
[Sitara gasps]

294
00:15:57,670 --> 00:15:59,420
[CHIEFTAIN] The solar eclipse?

295
00:15:59,470 --> 00:16:01,300
Oh, yes,
there certainly was one.

296
00:16:01,340 --> 00:16:02,880
During the hour
of the snake.

297
00:16:03,340 --> 00:16:06,550
About seven-tenths of the sun
must have been obscured.

298
00:16:06,600 --> 00:16:09,430
-[SITARA] Mm... I, too.
-[ALL] Huh?

299
00:16:09,480 --> 00:16:11,350
[SITARA] I saw eclipse.

300
00:16:11,390 --> 00:16:15,400
On that day, travel passing
close to Samarkand.

301
00:16:15,440 --> 00:16:17,400
When sun began to hide.

302
00:16:17,940 --> 00:16:20,690
The sky grew slightly dark.

303
00:16:20,740 --> 00:16:24,320
And the sun, maybe six
in ten parts became hidden.

304
00:16:24,360 --> 00:16:25,870
[BOTH] Huh?

305
00:16:27,160 --> 00:16:29,750
[SITARA] Crescent shadows
through the trees.

306
00:16:30,160 --> 00:16:31,960
A solar eclipse.

307
00:16:32,790 --> 00:16:35,540
Master Muhammad might've kept
a record of this.

308
00:16:36,080 --> 00:16:38,840
But I don't even know
the exact time right now.

309
00:16:38,880 --> 00:16:40,210
Mm? [gasps softly]

310
00:16:42,380 --> 00:16:45,260
That time, light from sun...

311
00:16:46,430 --> 00:16:50,600
shone onto poles of the roof.
I saw, in the tent.

312
00:16:50,640 --> 00:16:51,890
Oh.

313
00:16:52,060 --> 00:16:54,230
[CHINQAI] That would signal
the hour of the dragon.

314
00:16:54,270 --> 00:16:57,020
Does that mean the eclipse
started later around here

315
00:16:57,060 --> 00:16:58,110
than where you were?

316
00:16:58,320 --> 00:17:00,400
When Master Changchun
witnessed the eclipse,

317
00:17:00,440 --> 00:17:02,230
he was staying near
the Kherlen River

318
00:17:02,280 --> 00:17:03,360
off to the east.

319
00:17:03,400 --> 00:17:04,490
That was midday.

320
00:17:04,530 --> 00:17:05,870
Even later, then.

321
00:17:07,490 --> 00:17:10,790
[INTERPRETER] He was still able
to see the stars in the sky.

322
00:17:10,830 --> 00:17:14,250
[CHIEFTAIN] Hm. So there are
different degrees of eclipsing?

323
00:17:15,120 --> 00:17:16,540
[CHANGCHUN] Hmm.

324
00:17:16,580 --> 00:17:18,500
[INTERPRETER]
"During a solar eclipse,

325
00:17:18,540 --> 00:17:21,010
the moon casts its shadow
over the sun.

326
00:17:21,050 --> 00:17:22,710
-One could liken it...
-[Sitara gasps]

327
00:17:22,760 --> 00:17:25,630
...to obstructing this flame
with a fan."

328
00:17:25,930 --> 00:17:27,930
-[Changchun mumbles]
-"From where I sit,

329
00:17:27,970 --> 00:17:29,470
the flame is not visible,

330
00:17:29,510 --> 00:17:32,020
but the rest of you see
a different picture."

331
00:17:32,850 --> 00:17:36,690
The world is indeed
a truly wide place.

332
00:17:37,900 --> 00:17:39,020
[cattle bleating]

333
00:17:39,060 --> 00:17:40,360
[CHINQAI] I'm sorry.

334
00:17:40,520 --> 00:17:42,650
I didn't realize that
you weren't part of their group.

335
00:17:42,690 --> 00:17:45,150
-[SITARA] Huh?
-My name is Chinqai.

336
00:17:45,190 --> 00:17:46,700
I'm a Uyghur as well.

337
00:17:46,740 --> 00:17:48,700
Though I was able
to pick up a bit of Persian

338
00:17:48,740 --> 00:17:50,160
back in my peddling days.

339
00:17:51,240 --> 00:17:54,200
Oh, my name is Fatima.
It is nice to meet you.

340
00:17:54,250 --> 00:17:55,410
I am traveling east

341
00:17:55,750 --> 00:17:58,120
so that I may someday serve
the consort of Prince Tolui.

342
00:17:58,500 --> 00:18:00,790
Is that so?
For Princess Sorghaghtani?

343
00:18:00,840 --> 00:18:01,840
[Sitara exhales]

344
00:18:01,880 --> 00:18:04,090
So you're her
lady-in-waiting, eh?

345
00:18:04,130 --> 00:18:05,130
That makes sense,

346
00:18:05,300 --> 00:18:06,840
considering the cleverness
you displayed.

347
00:18:07,430 --> 00:18:09,090
That's kind of you to say.

348
00:18:09,930 --> 00:18:11,510
And well, um...

349
00:18:11,850 --> 00:18:13,520
If I may ask,

350
00:18:13,560 --> 00:18:16,140
how old is the master sage,
exactly?

351
00:18:17,060 --> 00:18:18,100
Hmm.

352
00:18:18,520 --> 00:18:21,020
[CHINQAI] So you wish to know
more about immortality?

353
00:18:21,360 --> 00:18:22,690
Well, my apologies.

354
00:18:22,730 --> 00:18:25,070
Unfortunately, I can't reveal
his secret to you

355
00:18:25,110 --> 00:18:27,110
before the Great Khan learns it.

356
00:18:28,570 --> 00:18:32,120
[SITARA] No. I have no interest
in becoming immortal.

357
00:18:32,160 --> 00:18:34,080
-[CHINQAI] Hm?
-There was a story in a book

358
00:18:34,120 --> 00:18:35,830
that I read when I was younger.

359
00:18:36,540 --> 00:18:40,380
It was about a man who traveled
to India in search of longevity.

360
00:18:40,420 --> 00:18:41,790
But the men he found there

361
00:18:41,840 --> 00:18:44,090
took care of their fathers
and grandfathers

362
00:18:44,130 --> 00:18:46,970
who were too elderly
to even stand.

363
00:18:47,010 --> 00:18:48,220
And also...

364
00:18:48,260 --> 00:18:49,630
My great-grandfather,

365
00:18:50,130 --> 00:18:51,800
I must cover him
with this sheet,

366
00:18:51,850 --> 00:18:53,560
lest the rats nibble
away at him.

367
00:18:53,600 --> 00:18:56,640
But he is still alive,
and that is a blessing.

368
00:18:57,310 --> 00:18:58,600
[SITARA] Upon hearing this,

369
00:18:58,640 --> 00:19:01,140
the man no longer
sought an extended life.

370
00:19:02,770 --> 00:19:05,730
Instead of defying Allah
with immortality,

371
00:19:05,780 --> 00:19:09,070
it is far better to welcome
a healthy and natural death.

372
00:19:09,110 --> 00:19:11,070
That was the moral of the tale.

373
00:19:11,120 --> 00:19:12,320
[Chinqai chuckles]

374
00:19:12,570 --> 00:19:14,280
You know, on second thought,

375
00:19:14,330 --> 00:19:16,240
Master Changchun
did divulge it to me.

376
00:19:16,290 --> 00:19:18,830
-[SITARA] Uh?
-The secret to immortality.

377
00:19:19,710 --> 00:19:20,920
[Sitara breathes uneasily]

378
00:19:20,960 --> 00:19:23,090
You seem like
a very bright girl,

379
00:19:23,130 --> 00:19:25,710
so I'll make an exception
and tell you.

380
00:19:26,210 --> 00:19:28,970
The secret to immortality is...

381
00:19:29,010 --> 00:19:30,470
[Sitara whimpers]

382
00:19:31,590 --> 00:19:32,890
Nonexistent.

383
00:19:32,930 --> 00:19:35,930
We must all welcome
a healthy and natural death.

384
00:19:36,260 --> 00:19:37,560
[SITARA] Uh?

385
00:19:37,890 --> 00:19:38,930
[gasps softly]

386
00:19:39,270 --> 00:19:42,190
Seems sages can be found
in the East and the West.

387
00:19:42,810 --> 00:19:46,400
Our empire may be mighty,
but it's also young.

388
00:19:46,440 --> 00:19:49,230
There are many things
we have yet to learn.

389
00:19:49,280 --> 00:19:52,400
Fatima, such wisdom as yours
is something

390
00:19:52,450 --> 00:19:54,530
our people desperately need.

391
00:19:56,280 --> 00:19:57,330
[SITARA] Uh...

392
00:19:57,370 --> 00:19:58,790
[rain pouring]

393
00:20:05,840 --> 00:20:07,920
[SHIRA] A path to success
based on merit

394
00:20:07,960 --> 00:20:09,300
has opened up to us.

395
00:20:10,380 --> 00:20:13,760
[SITARA] I don't know.
What can someone like me do?

396
00:20:14,590 --> 00:20:17,720
That's why I'm leaving here
on a journey.

397
00:20:18,930 --> 00:20:20,980
[SITARA]
Thinking back to that time,

398
00:20:22,560 --> 00:20:25,810
why did I feel such frustration?

399
00:20:27,110 --> 00:20:28,690
[wind howling]

400
00:20:33,650 --> 00:20:35,570
Keep walking!
We're almost there!

401
00:20:35,620 --> 00:20:37,620
[all breathe heavily]

402
00:20:39,540 --> 00:20:40,950
[soldier sighs]

403
00:20:42,160 --> 00:20:43,460
We made it!

404
00:20:43,500 --> 00:20:45,420
[Sitara pants]

405
00:20:48,800 --> 00:20:50,550
[SITARA] Uh? [gasps]

406
00:20:53,630 --> 00:20:56,680
According to the map
by Al-Biruni I once saw,

407
00:20:57,430 --> 00:21:00,890
I now find myself
at the world's edge.

408
00:21:06,900 --> 00:21:07,940
[wind blows]

409
00:21:07,980 --> 00:21:09,440
[ALL] Cold!

410
00:21:09,480 --> 00:21:11,900
-I can't believe this chill!
-I'm freezing!

411
00:21:12,780 --> 00:21:14,490
[LADY-IN-WAITING] Put these on.

412
00:21:14,530 --> 00:21:17,070
Welcome to
Princess Sorghaghtani's orda.

413
00:21:17,120 --> 00:21:19,280
Her Highness made certain
to supply coats

414
00:21:19,330 --> 00:21:21,410
and warm gers
in preparation for your arrival.

415
00:21:21,450 --> 00:21:23,000
[all gasp]

416
00:21:23,410 --> 00:21:24,960
[indistinct chatter]

417
00:21:29,420 --> 00:21:31,590
Are any of you girls named
Fatima?

418
00:21:31,630 --> 00:21:33,010
[SITARA] Huh?

419
00:21:37,340 --> 00:21:40,390
[LADY] Princess Sorghaghtani.
I've brought the girl.

420
00:21:46,730 --> 00:21:48,400
[SORGHAGHTANI]
You have my thanks...

421
00:21:49,360 --> 00:21:51,280
...for making the long journey
all the way here in this cold.

422
00:21:52,360 --> 00:21:53,480
[Sitara gasps]

423
00:21:54,860 --> 00:21:56,110
How exciting!

424
00:21:56,320 --> 00:21:58,530
You're the one who will be
teaching me this book, yes?

425
00:21:59,200 --> 00:22:01,740
[Sitara gasps]

426
00:22:02,490 --> 00:22:05,160
[♪ closing theme song playing]

427
00:23:32,580 --> 00:23:34,790
[SITARA] Next time, "Otchigin."

